 "figures": {
  "scores": {
   "title": "Every era against three fixed eras",
   "caption": "Each line is one fixed era: the share every other era's brains held against its brains, the mean of two contests of 100 iterations, one on each era's world. The grey band is 0.5 plus or minus twice the spread of the fixed eras played against themselves: a score inside it could be noise.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** Experiment 28's 240 contests (`python3 gol_lab.py run E28`): every 2,500th era of Experiment 18's two worlds played to 50,000 iterations (`B2-10000-4af4a3-s005`, `-s006`) against the eras of 2,500, 10,000 and 25,000, each pair once on each era's world (coin 17), and each of those three eras against itself four times (coins 17 to 20).\n\n**Data.** From each trial's `result.json`.\n\n1. For each pair of eras, the later era's share of the agents after 100 iterations, the mean of its two contests; an earlier era's score against a later one is 1 less that.\n2. The band: the standard deviation of the twelve contests of an era against itself, twice on either side of 0.5.\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py eras`."
  },
  "ratings": {
   "title": "Each era's rating, from every contest",
   "caption": "One rating per era and world, fitted to every contest of that world by least squares on the log-odds of the scores (Bradley\u2013Terry): +100 means the era's brains beat the brains of 2,500 about 64 times in a hundred.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** Experiment 28's 240 contests (`python3 gol_lab.py run E28`): every 2,500th era of Experiment 18's two worlds played to 50,000 iterations (`B2-10000-4af4a3-s005`, `-s006`) against the eras of 2,500, 10,000 and 25,000, each pair once on each era's world (coin 17), and each of those three eras against itself four times (coins 17 to 20).\n\n**Data.** From each trial's `result.json`.\n\n1. For each pair of eras, the mean share of the later era over its two contests.\n2. Fit one rating per era so that the log-odds of every score is the difference of the two ratings, the era of 2,500 at 0 (`gol_tournament.ratings`); shown in Elo points.\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py eras`."
  },
  "room": {
   "title": "What a best reply would gain, era by era",
   "caption": "Each line is a family of long worlds: for a sample of up to 300 agents of each world holding three tokens or more, the nodes it would hold after one game by its best reply to the others' play, less what its own play holds, averaged. A shaded band, where a family has several worlds, runs from its lowest world to its highest; the line is the median. A population at an equilibrium would score 0.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** The lab's long worlds: Experiment 18's two worlds played to 50,000 iterations (`B2-10000-4af4a3-s005`, `-s006`, every 2,500), Experiment 19's ten to 20,000 (`B2-10000-bcc1bc-s001` \u2026 `-s010`), Experiment 17's to 10,000 (`B2-10000-4af4a3-s001` \u2026 `-s010` but the two above) and Experiment 16's B2 worlds to 2,000 (`B2-10000-s001` \u2026 `-s010`), at the iterations their archives keep.\n\n**Data.** From the worlds the archives keep.\n\n1. Load the world at the iteration and play one game, so its messages are fresh.\n2. Draw the random inputs 8 times and turn every brain's outputs into stakes as the engine does: the others' play, as 8 draws.\n3. For up to 300 agents with three tokens or more, score its own play and the best of a set of replies (everything home; the cheapest way to keep its node and take the cheapest neighbours; taking without defending) by the nodes it holds after the game, averaged over the 8 draws (`python3 research/oee/checks/exploitability.py RUN:ITERATION \u2026 --draws 8 --sample 300`).\n4. The gain is the best reply's score less the own play's, averaged over the agents.\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py eras`."
  }
